  "id": "educateos.io/english-9-a-threshold-of-my-own",
  "language": "en",
  "readings": [
    {
      "attribution": "Written for EducateOS.",
      "body": "A personal essay is a true story told by the person who lived it, shaped so that a reader understands what it meant. These techniques help.\n\nStart inside a moment. Begin with a specific scene, not a summary of your life. \"I was nine and standing on a milk crate\" pulls a reader in faster than \"I have always helped my family.\"\n\nShow, then reflect. Let the reader see and hear the scene first: actions, dialogue, sensory details. Then step back and reflect on what it meant. The best essays move between the two.\n\nSlow down the important moment. Pacing is a choice. Spend one sentence on five years and five paragraphs on five minutes, if those five minutes are the threshold.\n\nUse telling details. One precise detail (a name tag, a pen on a chain, a shaking hand) does more than a paragraph of adjectives.\n\nLet dialogue do work. Use dialogue sparingly, for lines that reveal character or turn the story. Punctuate it correctly and start a new paragraph for each new speaker.\n\nFind the turn. Most personal essays have a moment where the writer's understanding changes. In Unit 2 we called it a threshold.\n\nEnd with reflection, not a moral. Avoid \"and that taught me to always...\" Instead, show where the experience has left you and how you see things differently today. Often the ending echoes the beginning, changed.\n\nRevise in passes. Read once for structure (does every scene build toward the turn?), once for detail (where can I be more specific?), and once aloud for sound and correctness.",
      "id": "narrative-craft-notes",
      "title": "Narrative Craft Notes"
    }
  ],
