      "attribution": "Written for EducateOS.",
      "body": "The Declaration of Independence (1776). Drafted mainly by Thomas Jefferson and adopted by the Continental Congress, it announced that the thirteen colonies were breaking from Great Britain. Its second paragraph states a theory of government: people have natural rights, and governments exist to protect them. When it was signed, slavery was legal in every one of the colonies, women could not vote, and in most colonies men without property could not either.\n\nThe Declaration of Sentiments (1848). About three hundred people gathered at Seneca Falls, New York, for the first women's rights convention in the United States. Elizabeth Cady Stanton modeled the convention's declaration closely on Jefferson's, sentence by sentence, but replaced King George with \"man\" and added \"and women.\" One hundred people signed it, including Frederick Douglass. Its call for women's right to vote was the most controversial resolution; it passed narrowly.\n\n\"What to the Slave Is the Fourth of July?\" (1852). Frederick Douglass had escaped slavery in 1838 and become one of the most famous speakers in the country. Invited to speak at an Independence Day event in Rochester, New York, he chose to speak on July 5. He praised the founders and the principles of the Declaration, then turned to his audience and asked what those principles meant to four million enslaved people.\n\nThe Gettysburg Address (1863). In July 1863, the Battle of Gettysburg left more than 7,000 soldiers dead in three days. That November, Abraham Lincoln spoke at the dedication of a national cemetery there. The main speaker talked for two hours; Lincoln spoke for about two minutes. \"Four score and seven years ago\" counts back eighty-seven years, from 1863 to 1776: Lincoln dated the nation's birth from the Declaration, not from the Constitution.",
