      "body": "My childhood is a coat I still keep on the hook,\ntwo sizes small, the zipper bitten through.\nI do not wear it. Some mornings I just look.\n\nMy mother's kitchen is a country with one rule:\nwe eat together, even when we fight.\nI learned its anthem: dishes, bread, the stool.\n\nMy future is a hallway with the lights turned low.\nI hear a door. I cannot see the knob.\nI walk toward it anyway. I go.\n\nAnd this, tonight, is mine: a window and a moon,\na notebook with its pages mostly white.\nI write one line. I'll write the next one soon.",
