    {
      "attribution": "Written for EducateOS. An original very short story.",
      "body": "At 11:40 p.m. the laundromat on Fifth Street is empty except for Dani and the sound of the dryers. She is sixteen, and it is her first night closing alone.\n\nAt 11:42 she counts the drawer. Eighty-three dollars in quarters and bills. She counts it again, because her aunt, who owns the place, counts everything twice.\n\nAt 11:47 a man comes in with a garbage bag of clothes. He is wearing a uniform from the hospital across the street, and his eyes look like he has not closed them in a day. \"I know you close at midnight,\" he says. \"I'm sorry. It's the only clean thing I have for tomorrow.\"\n\nThe rule is posted on the door: last wash at 11:00. Her aunt wrote it. Her aunt says rules are what keep a small business from becoming a charity.\n\nAt 11:48 Dani says, \"Machine six is the fastest.\"\n\nAt 11:49 she texts her aunt: \"Running late. All fine.\" She deletes it. She types it again. She sends it.\n\nAt 12:15 a.m. the man is asleep in a plastic chair, his head against the window. Dani moves his clothes to a dryer without waking him. She finds, in a shirt pocket, a folded drawing in crayon: a stick figure in a hospital uniform, and the words GOOD JOB DAD.\n\nShe puts it back exactly as she found it.\n\nAt 12:52 a.m. he wakes, embarrassed, and tries to pay her extra. She takes only the price of the wash.\n\nAt 1:05 a.m. she locks the door. Her phone shows one message from her aunt, sent an hour ago: \"Everything ok?\"\n\nAt 1:06 a.m. Dani writes back: \"Yes. Tell you tomorrow. I think I broke a rule the right way.\"\n\nShe walks home under the streetlights, counting nothing.",
